fips-initramfs for Debian
=========================

This package starts a FIPS mesh node during early boot and binds dropbear
to the node's fips0 address. An operator can then reach the machine over
the mesh and run cryptroot-unlock to unlock an encrypted root filesystem.


The binaries come with the package
----------------------------------

The package carries the fips daemon and fipsctl in
/usr/lib/fips-initramfs/bin, so it needs nothing else installed and
nothing done by hand. They are taken from a published FIPS release at
build time and verified against the checksums that release publishes;
/usr/share/fips-initramfs/fips-version records which release.

The booted system runs no FIPS daemon and has no fips0. The node exists
only inside the initramfs, for the length of the unlock.

Install the fips package as well if you want a daemon on the running
system. /usr/bin is then searched first, so the node in the initramfs is
the same build as the daemon the booted system runs. That is the only
difference it makes here.

FIPS_BINDIR in /etc/fips-initramfs/fips-initramfs.conf replaces the
search rather than extending it: a directory named there and found
wanting is an error, not a reason to quietly use a different daemon.


Setting it up
-------------

1. Install the package. It asks for a bootstrap peer: an npub, a
   transport and an address. Leave the npub blank to use the FIPS public
   test mesh, which is what the shipped configuration carries and which
   accepts inbound peering from any npub.

   The install then generates an identity keypair in /etc/fips-initramfs
   and prints the name this machine unlocks at. Record that name.

2. Review /etc/fips-initramfs/fips.yaml. The prompt sets one peer; more
   than one is better, and the shipped defaults show the shape. This
   file is created by the install and is never rewritten afterwards, so
   edit it freely.

3. Nothing, if you answered the SSH key question during the install.

   The install asks for the public key allowed to unlock this machine and
   writes it to /etc/dropbear/initramfs/authorized_keys. It asks only
   when that file names no key, and it never overwrites one that is
   there. Answer with the whole line from a .pub file, and write the key
   on its own with no options in front of it; the package adds the forced
   command described below.

   Add further keys to that file yourself. It belongs to no package:
   dropbear-initramfs ships only dropbear.conf and documents this file in
   /usr/share/doc/dropbear-initramfs/README.initramfs.

   If you left the question blank, the install says so and this machine
   has no remote unlock until you put a key there and run
   update-initramfs -u.

4. Run: update-initramfs -u

   The install already built an image, so this is only needed after you
   change something: another key, a different peer, or a setting.

5. Test the result before you rely on it. Reboot with console access, and
   confirm that you can reach <npub>.fips over the mesh and that
   cryptroot-unlock completes the boot. Connect to the name, never to the
   bare address; see below for why.


A second install asks nothing, and that is deliberate
-----------------------------------------------------

Installing over an existing install is silent: neither question comes back
and the machine keeps the identity it had. Three things account for it,
and each is wanted.

  - debconf remembers the answers, so the questions are not asked again.
  - The key question is asked only when authorized_keys names no key, and
    the answer is never written over one that is there.
  - postinst generates a keypair only when there is none, so the npub is
    stable and a name you published keeps resolving.

The consequence is worth knowing before you go looking for a fault:
**to see what a first install does, purge first.** dpkg -P removes
/etc/fips-initramfs and the identity with it, and the next install asks
everything and generates a new npub. Reinstalling without purging is the
right thing on a machine you are keeping and the wrong thing on a machine
you are testing.


The session is forced to cryptroot-unlock
----------------------------------------

An SSH session into the initramfs runs cryptroot-unlock and cannot open
a shell. That is the default and it needs nothing from you:

  ssh -i your-key root@<npub>.fips

Dropbear runs a forced command whatever the client asks for, so naming
another command has no effect either. The reason is the one the Security
section gives: the initramfs shell is a root shell with the mesh already
attached, so an unrestricted session is a foothold on the mesh and not
only on this machine.

The package makes the change on the copy of authorized_keys inside the
generated image. The file in /etc/dropbear/initramfs is never touched,
so it stays yours and stays what dropbear-initramfs documents.

Two cases are left alone, and update-initramfs reports each one:

  - A key that already carries its own options. Prepending to it could
    duplicate a command= you set or defeat a restriction, so the line is
    left exactly as written and gets no forced command.

  - Every key, when cryptroot-unlock is not in the image. That is what
    happens without cryptsetup-initramfs, and forcing a command that
    does not exist would close every session at once.

To turn it off, set FIPS_FORCE_UNLOCK_COMMAND=no in
/etc/fips-initramfs/fips-initramfs.conf and run update-initramfs -u. Do
that while bringing a machine up. A shell in the initramfs is how a
failed unlock gets diagnosed, and there is no other way in once the
machine is remote and locked.


The configuration file is created, not shipped
---------------------------------------------

/etc/fips-initramfs/fips.yaml is not a dpkg conffile. The package ships
/usr/share/fips-initramfs/fips.yaml.example, and the install copies it
into place, substituting the peer from the prompt, only when no file is
there already.

So an upgrade never produces a three-way merge prompt for it, never
clobbers an operator-edited or configuration-management-rendered file,
and never revisits the peer question once the file exists. A later
dpkg-reconfigure will ask again but will change nothing; edit the file.

Purging the package removes it, along with the identity.

The one file that IS a conffile is fips-initramfs.conf, which carries
only FIPS_BINDIR.


What the bootstrap peer prompt accepts
--------------------------------------

The install asks for the peer in three parts. All three have to make
sense together, and the answers are used only when the configuration
file does not already exist.

  Bootstrap peer npub:       an npub1... string, or blank
  Bootstrap peer transport:  udp (the default), tcp, tor or ethernet
  Bootstrap peer address:    host:port

The npub is the peer's identity, exactly as FIPS prints it. Leave it
blank to keep the shipped defaults, which are the FIPS public test mesh.

The transport is normally udp. Only udp and tcp make sense this early in
a boot: tor and ethernet are accepted, but tor needs a running Tor and
ethernet needs a peer on the same segment.

The address is where to dial that peer, as host:port. Either a hostname
or a literal address will do. A hostname needs a resolver, which the
initramfs takes from its DHCP lease; a literal address needs none.

The npub and the address are both required. Giving one without the
other is not a partial answer: the install says so and falls back to the
shipped defaults.

  fips-initramfs: an npub and an address are both needed to
  fips-initramfs: configure a peer. Using the public test mesh.

An answer produces one peer, at priority 10, with connect_policy
auto_connect:

  peers:
    - npub: "npub1..."
      addresses:
        - transport: udp
          addr: "peer.example.net:2121"
          priority: 10
      connect_policy: auto_connect

The prompt cannot express more than that. A second peer, a second
address for the same peer, or an alias is an edit to
/etc/fips-initramfs/fips.yaml afterwards, followed by update-initramfs
-u. The shipped fips.yaml.example is the worked example of the fuller
form.


Peer addresses and their order
------------------------------

Each peer takes a list of addresses, tried in order of "priority",
lowest first. The shipped defaults give each peer a hostname at 10 and a
literal address at 20.

Both are worth having. The hostname form works in an initramfs: the
resolver comes from the DHCP lease, and a machine configured with a
hostname and no address reached its peer and became its tree child. But
a name needs DNS to have come up, and an address does not survive the
peer moving. Neither alone covers both.


What the node adds to the image
-------------------------------

The daemon and its configuration make the generated initramfs about 10 MB
larger. Measured on Ubuntu 24.04: 76 MB without the node and 85.8 MB with
it, a rise of roughly 13 per cent.

That is comfortable on a normal /boot and it is not on a small one.
Several kernels are kept, so the cost is paid once per kernel. Check the
free space on /boot before installing this on a machine with a /boot of
a few hundred megabytes.


Editing the configuration does not rebuild the image
----------------------------------------------------

A dpkg file trigger rebuilds the initramfs when a package writes into a
watched path. It does not fire when you edit a file by hand. So an edit
to /etc/fips-initramfs/fips.yaml has no effect until you run
update-initramfs -u.

The symptom of forgetting appears one reboot later, and it is silent.


A stale bootstrap peer makes the machine unreachable
-----------------------------------------------------

The node in the initramfs uses static peers only. Nostr-mediated
rendezvous needs DNS, a plausible wall clock and relay reachability. None
of those hold this early in boot.

If every configured peer has moved, the node converges with nobody. There
is no diagnostic on the console and no signal as to why. The only
recovery is physical access to the console passphrase prompt.

Configure several always-on peers with public endpoints. This is the
mitigation the package offers, and it is the only one.


Console entry always survives
-----------------------------

The wait for the mesh is bounded. If the mesh does not come up, the boot
falls through to the ordinary cryptsetup passphrase prompt on the
console. Every failure in this path behaves that way: no DHCP lease, no
route to a peer, a peer that moved, a rejected clock, or a tun module
that a kernel upgrade left out of the image.

Never disable console passphrase entry on a machine that uses this
package.

A graphical console changes nothing here. /lib/cryptsetup/askpass is one
binary that asks through plymouth when plymouth is running and creates
and watches /lib/cryptsetup/passfifo either way, and cryptroot-unlock
finds its target by looking for the process holding that FIFO rather
than by looking at the console. Tested with plymouth installed and
"splash" on the kernel command line: plymouthd was running in the
initramfs, plymouth --ping answered, and the unlock over the mesh
succeeded unchanged.

What would break it is a keyscript= in crypttab that is not askpass.
There is then no FIFO, and cryptroot-unlock has nothing to write to.


When the image is built without a FIPS node
------------------------------------------

update-initramfs prints a warning and builds the image anyway whenever
something the node needs is missing: no bootstrap peers, no fips and
fipsctl to be found, no identity key, or a fipsctl too old to derive an
address. The image is a plain dropbear-initramfs image, and unlock is
console-only until the cause is fixed and update-initramfs -u is run.

The build is never failed for this. A failing initramfs hook leaves
initramfs-tools half-configured and aborts whatever apt run it belonged
to, which turns a misconfigured unlock into a broken package system. An
image with no FIPS node still boots and still asks for the passphrase on
the console.

Watch for those warnings during an upgrade. They are the only signal,
and the symptom otherwise appears at the next remote boot.


What the boot scripts check
---------------------------

The image carries the mesh address the node is expected to come up at,
and the name that address belongs to. The premount script compares the
address on fips0 against the expected one and warns when they differ. On
success it prints both:

  Success: FIPS node up at npub1....fips (fd13:7a57:...)

The name is the part to connect to. The address is there to be checked
against, and dialling it directly never works; see below.

That check exists because the two most likely failures both leave a
healthy daemon running:

  - A missing or misnamed key file does not fail the boot. The daemon
    generates a new keypair and runs normally under a new identity, at an
    address nobody is connecting to.

  - tun.enabled set to false, or a tun module absent from the image,
    gives a running daemon with no fips0 at all.

Neither a process liveness check nor the presence of fips0 can see the
first one. Read /run/fips.log from a console shell when a warning fires.


Script ordering
---------------

The premount script is named a_fips so that it sorts ahead of the
dropbear script. It has to, because dropbear binds to an address this
package creates.

initramfs-tools orders boot scripts with tsort over their declared
PREREQ lines, and falls back to the sorted filename list for scripts with
no relationship. The dropbear script cannot declare a dependency on a
package it does not know about, so the ordering rests on that fallback.

If a future version of initramfs-tools or dropbear-initramfs reorders
them, dropbear binds before the address exists and remote unlock stops
working. The console prompt still works. This is a known and accepted
fragility.


Security
--------

The node key, the dropbear host key and authorized_keys all sit
unencrypted in /boot. Anyone who can read the image can impersonate this
unlock node end to end and phish the passphrase.

That exposure is not new. dropbear-initramfs already has it with its host
key. What FIPS changes is reach: the impersonating node no longer needs
to sit on your network path, because it can present itself on the mesh
from anywhere.

The mesh transport does not protect against this. Three things narrow it:

  - The peer ACL, which admits or refuses peers by npub.
  - The default-deny nftables baseline the fips package ships at
    /etc/fips/fips.nft, which permits only TCP 22 from the mesh.
  - UMASK=0077 for the generated image. dropbear-initramfs already sets
    this through its own initramfs-tools conf-hook, so it holds wherever
    this package is installed.

The session itself is restricted by default. See "The session is forced
to cryptroot-unlock" above: an SSH session into the initramfs runs that
command and cannot open a shell.

The rest of the restrictions are yours to add, in
/etc/dropbear/initramfs/authorized_keys:

  no-port-forwarding,no-agent-forwarding,no-X11-forwarding ssh-ed25519 AAAA... you@host

A key line that already carries options is left exactly as it is, so a
line like that one gets no forced command and update-initramfs says so.
Write the command yourself when you add options:

  command="/usr/bin/cryptroot-unlock",no-port-forwarding,no-agent-forwarding,no-X11-forwarding ssh-ed25519 AAAA... you@host

Whether to add no-pty depends on how you unlock. It is safe for a piped
passphrase and it stops an interactive prompt from being drawn.


Removing and purging
--------------------

dpkg -r keeps the identity in /etc/fips-initramfs and rebuilds the image
without a FIPS node, so reinstalling brings back the same mesh address.
dpkg -P removes the identity along with the rest of that directory.

The binaries in /usr/lib/fips-initramfs/bin belong to the package and go
with it, whether it is removed or purged.


The FIPS version floor
----------------------

This package needs fipsctl's offline address command, which first shipped
in FIPS 0.5.0. The build refuses a release older than that, so the copies
the package carries always clear the floor.

The floor is not enforced on a machine that installs the fips package,
because /usr/bin is searched first and nothing here decides what version
goes there. An older fipsctl has no address command; the image is then
built without a FIPS node and update-initramfs says why. The bundled
copies are not tried as a fallback in that case.

The two packages are not built from one source tree, and the FIPS
configuration schema is not stable, so a newer FIPS can introduce a
schema break that nothing detects until boot. The release this package
bundles is the one it was last tested against, and it moves by hand after
someone re-runs the boot test.


Unlocking
---------

Connect to the recorded name. The session runs cryptroot-unlock on its
own, unless you turned that off. If it reports that there is nothing to
unlock, the console prompt has not been reached yet. Retry rather than
assume the setup is broken.

Several encrypted devices, including a separately encrypted swap or
resume device, are prompted for in sequence and are handled in the same
session.

Send the passphrase with no trailing newline:

  printf '%s' 'your passphrase' | ssh -i key root@<npub>.fips

On a terminal cryptroot-unlock reads the passphrase and strips the
newline. Given a pipe it copies stdin into the askpass FIFO verbatim, so
a trailing newline becomes part of the passphrase and the unlock fails
with "bad password or options". Three such failures exhaust the
cryptsetup retry count for that boot.


Always connect to the name, never the address
---------------------------------------------

Use <npub>.fips. Do not use the bare IPv6 address, even though the
install prints one and the boot scripts compare against it.

A mesh address has to be resolved to tree coordinates before anything can
reach it, and resolving the .fips name through the FIPS DNS responder is
what drives that resolution. Given a bare address there is nothing to
resolve, so the lookup is never made and the connection fails with no
route. Measured: ninety seconds of pinging a bare address never got
through, while resolving the name and then connecting worked on the first
try from a cold cache.

So the machine you connect FROM needs a FIPS daemon with its DNS
responder enabled and the system resolver pointed at it. That is what the
fips package's fips-dns.service arranges. This is a property of the
connecting machine, not of the one being unlocked: the node in the
initramfs has dns.enabled set to false, because nothing in an initramfs
resolves .fips names.

The install prints the npub for this reason. Record it. Once the machine
is locked there is no way to read it off.

  ssh -i your-key root@<npub>.fips

